 "drivingQuestion": "How does the court determine that most of the city was subverted?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "Ulla's procedure replaces Rav Yehuda's",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "sanhedrin.112a.4",
   "daf": "112a",
   "sentenceId": 3,
   "he": "עַד שֶׁיּוּדַח רוּבָּהּ הֵיכִי עָבְדִינַן אָמַר רַב יְהוּדָה דָּנִין וְחוֹבְשִׁין דָּנִין וְחוֹבְשִׁין אֲמַר לֵיהּ עוּלָּא נִמְצָא אַתָּה מְעַנֶּה אֶת דִּינָן שֶׁל אֵלּוּ אֶלָּא אָמַר עוּלָּא דָּנִין וְסוֹקְלִין דָּנִין וְסוֹקְלִין",
   "en": "§ The mishna teaches that a city is not deemed an idolatrous city «unless most of» the inhabitants of the city «are subverted.» The Gemara asks: «How do we act» in order to determine if most of the inhabitants have been subverted? «Rav Yehuda says:» The court «judges» each inhabitant suspected of idol worship, «and» if he is found liable «imprisons» him in a jail, and it «judges and imprisons» each succeeding idolater until a majority of the city has been convicted. «Ulla said to him:» That will «result in you delaying justice for those» already sentenced and awaiting execution, and it is prohibited to delay justice. «Rather, Ulla says:» The court «judges» each inhabitant «and stones» him if he is found liable, then «judges and stones» each succeeding idolater until half of the city is found guilty, and all future convicted idolaters are executed by the sword."
  }
 ],
